course: biology
lesson: 362
title: Eyes and ears deeper: glasses, colour blindness, tears, earwax and dizziness
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Your eyes and ears are not just windows and holes: they are clever machines with lenses, wipers, drains, pipes and tiny spirit levels, and the brain quietly fixes their faults so you never notice.
explain: Think of spinning round in the playground and stopping: the world keeps turning because fluid inside your ears is still swirling, like tea that keeps going round after you stop stirring.
explain: When a part works a little differently, such as an eyeball that is too long or colour cells that mix up red and green, things look blurry or the same, and glasses or a few tricks help.
explain: Many of these parts work together, so when eyes and ears tell the brain different stories, as when you read in a moving car, you can feel sick.
fact: In short sight the eyeball is a little too long, so pictures of far things come into focus in front of the back of the eye, and a glasses lens bends the light so the focus lands in the right place.
fact: More children are short-sighted now than 50 years ago, and many scientists think too little time outdoors in daylight is part of the reason.
fact: After about 40 the lens inside the eye gets stiffer and cannot change shape enough to focus on close things, so almost everyone needs reading glasses one day.
fact: The eye has three kinds of colour cells, for red, green and blue light; in colour blindness one kind works differently, usually making some reds and greens hard to tell apart.
fact: About 1 boy in 12 is colour blind but only about 1 girl in 200, because of how the gene is passed on; a test of coloured dots with a hidden number finds it.
fact: Every eye has a blind spot where the nerve leaves and there are no light cells; the brain fills in the hole, and an octopus eye, wired the other way round, has none.
fact: Cutting an onion lets out a gas that stings the eye, and reflex tears wash it away; there are also everyday tears that keep eyes wet and tears when you feel sad or very happy.
fact: Tears drain through two tiny holes at the inner corner of each eye into the nose, which is why your nose runs when you cry.
fact: Most people blink about 15 to 20 times a minute, each blink spreading tears like a wiper; staring at a screen makes you blink much less, so eyes feel dry.
fact: Earwax traps dust, helps keep germs out and moves out by itself as you chew and talk; cotton buds often push it deeper, and one gene decides whether it is dry and flaky or wet and sticky.
fact: Deep in each ear are three curved tubes full of fluid, set at right angles like the corner of a box, that tell the brain when the head turns.
fact: In car sickness the inner ear feels the car moving but eyes on a page say everything is still; looking out of the front window at the horizon helps them agree.
fact: A thin tube joins each middle ear to the back of the throat to let air in and out; on a plane, swallowing, yawning or chewing opens it, and a cold can block it and make ear pain worse.
exercise: Why does a short-sighted child see far things blurry? | The eyeball is a little too long, so the focus lands in front of the back of the eye | The eye has too few tears | The child is colour blind | The eyelids are too heavy
exercise: What does a glasses lens do? | It bends the light a little so the focus lands in the right place | It slowly squashes the eyeball so the eye grows a little shorter each year | It adds extra colour cells to the eye | It stops you blinking
exercise: Why are more children short-sighted than 50 years ago, many scientists think? | Too little time outdoors in daylight is part of the reason | Children today eat far too many carrots, which change the eye | Wearing glasses makes the eyes grow worse | Children blink too often
exercise: Why does Granny hold her phone at arm's length to read it? | The lens in her eye has got stiff and cannot focus on close things | Her eyeballs have grown too long, so close things land in the wrong place | She has a bigger blind spot | Her tears have dried up
exercise: How many kinds of colour cells does a human eye have? | Three, for red, green and blue light | Just one kind, which sees all the colours | Seven, one for each colour of the rainbow | Two kinds, one for black and one for white
exercise: Which colours do colour-blind people most often mix up? | Some reds and greens | Only black and white, nothing else | Only the blues and the yellows | All colours, they see in grey
exercise: About how many boys are colour blind? | About 1 in 12 | About 1 in every 2 | About 1 in 1,000 | None, only girls are
exercise: Why does every eye have a blind spot? | The nerve leaves the eye there and there are no light cells | A tiny speck of dust always sits on that one part of the eye and blocks it | It is where tears are made | The eyelid covers that part
exercise: Why does cutting onions make you cry? | A gas from the onion stings the eye and tears wash it away | The sharp smell of onions makes you feel really sad inside | Onion juice blocks the tear drains | Onions are hot like chillies
exercise: Where do tears drain away to? | Through tiny holes at the inner corner of the eye into the nose | Into the ears, through a long thin tube that runs behind the cheek | Back into the brain | Out through the eyelashes
exercise: About how many times a minute do most people blink? | About 15 to 20 | Only about once or twice | About 200 or more | Never while awake
exercise: What happens to blinking when you stare at a screen? | You blink much less, so eyes get dry | You blink much more, so your eyes get wet | You slowly stop seeing some of the colours | Your blind spot grows bigger and bigger
exercise: What does earwax do? | Traps dust and helps keep germs out | Helps you hear the very high notes in music | Makes the ear grow bigger as you get older | Turns sounds into words you can understand
exercise: Why do doctors say not to poke cotton buds into the ear? | They often push wax deeper and can scratch the ear | Earwax is poisonous to touch with your fingers | Cotton makes the ear grow more wax | Cotton buds are too soft to clean anything at all inside an ear
exercise: Why does the room keep spinning after you stop turning round? | The fluid in the curved tubes of your ears keeps swirling | Your eyes keep turning round on their own for a minute or two | The blood in your feet is still moving | The floor really moves a little
exercise: How many curved fluid tubes are there in each ear? | Three | One | Eight | Ten
exercise: Why can reading in the back of a car make you feel sick? | Your ears feel moving but your eyes say still, and the brain gets mixed stories | The comic paper has a smell | The seat belt squeezes your tummy | Cars use up the air in the back
exercise: Why do your ears pop when a plane comes down? | A tube to the throat opens and lets air in to match the pressure | Earwax melts in the warm air of the plane and drips out of your ears | The eardrum breaks and mends | Your ears grow bigger in the air
test: «my mate's colour blind, so he sees everything in black and white like an old telly» What is right? | No, most colour-blind people see colours but mix up some reds and greens | Yes, colour-blind people see only grey, like a very old black and white film | Yes, but only at night | No, colour-blind people see no colours except blue
test: How could traffic lights help a colour-blind driver who cannot tell red from green? | Red is always in the same place, at the top, so the position tells him | Red lights always make a loud beeping noise so that drivers can hear them change | Green lights blink and red ones do not | Colour-blind people are not allowed near roads
test: Count them: a pirate in a cartoon has an eye patch over one eye. How many blind spots has he got in his open eyes? | 1 | 2 | 0 | 3
test: «why don't I see a hole in everything if each eye has a blind spot» What is the answer? | The brain fills the hole with what is around it, and the other eye covers it | There is no blind spot at all, it is just a myth | The blind spot only opens at night | We blink far too fast to ever notice the big dark hole in the middle of what we see
test: «earwax is gross, gonna dig it all out with a cotton bud every day» What would a doctor say? | Leave the inside alone; wax cleans itself out and protects the ear | Good idea, the deeper you dig the cleaner and healthier the ear will be | Use a pencil instead, it is harder | Wash it out with hot water daily
test: How might a ballet dancer spin many times without getting dizzy? | She fixes her eyes on one spot and whips her head round last | She closes both ears tightly with her fingers so the fluid cannot move | She holds her breath the whole time | She spins only on her left foot
test: In a cartoon, Jerry hits Tom on the head and stars and birds spin round him for ages. What makes a real person feel the world is spinning after they stop turning? | Fluid swirling on in the curved tubes of the inner ear | Little birds really flying round their head | Their brain falling asleep for a moment | Their eyes rolling right round in their heads
test: Why do drivers almost never get car sick? | They look ahead at the road, so their eyes and ears agree | Drivers have stronger ears than other people because they train them | The steering wheel stops sickness | Drivers hold their breath while driving
test: What would happen if your tear drains were blocked? | Tears would spill down your cheeks even when you are not crying | Your eyes would become totally dry and sore, because no tears could be made | You would never sneeze again | You would see in black and white
test: «got a stinking cold and flying tomorrow, whatever» Why might the plane hurt his ears more than usual? | A cold can block the tube that balances air in the middle ear | A cold makes his eardrums much thicker and stiffer than they should be | The plane flies higher for people with colds | Earwax melts in a cold
test: «nan says she never needed glasses so she never will, she's 60» What is most likely? | Almost everyone needs reading glasses one day as the eye's lens gets stiff | She is right, eyes never change once you are a grown-up, however old you get | Only children ever need glasses | Glasses are only for short sight
test: Count them: two friends stare at screens for one minute and blink 5 times each instead of about 15. How many blinks did they miss together? | 20 | 10 | 5 | 30
exam: Identify the principal cause of the condition known as short sight. | The eyeball is of excessive length, so distant images are focused in front of the retina | The lens of the eye is excessively stiff | The colour-sensitive cells are absent | The tear ducts are obstructed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the comparative frequency of colour blindness in boys and girls? | It is considerably more frequent among boys, approximately one in twelve | It is considerably more frequent among girls | It is equally frequent among boys and girls | It is confined entirely to adults
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the sensation of dizziness experienced upon cessation of rotation. | The fluid within the semicircular canals of the inner ear continues in motion | The eyeballs continue to rotate within their sockets | The blood within the feet continues to circulate in a circle | The ground beneath the subject undergoes rotation
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the practice of reading in the rear of a moving vehicle is apt to induce nausea. | The organs of balance perceive motion whilst the eyes report stillness | The paper of the book emits a nauseating odour | The seat belt compresses the stomach | The air within the vehicle is wholly consumed
